WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.670
INTRO:
10 years ago, Facebook was the up and coming

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:06.260
social media company that people couldn’t
get enough of.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:10.940
Mark Zuckerberg was the next child prodigy
like Bill Gates and Steve Jobs reaching the

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.280
billion dollar mark at just 23 years old.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.790
It seemed like he was going to build one of
the biggest companies in the world, and that’s

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:20.470
exactly what he did.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:24.680
But, people aren’t exactly happy with his
rise to the top.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:30.250
Facebook’s rise has been riddled with controversy
and privacy concerns, and this isn’t surprising

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:35.110
given that the company’s entire business
model revolves around selling data to advertisers.

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:41.039
In fact, things have gotten so bad that Yahoo
finance ranked Facebook as the worst company

00:00:41.039 --> 00:00:42.710
of 2021.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:47.050
Despite all this bad press that Facebook has
been receiving for years now, this negative

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.260
perception never really hurt their bottom
line.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:54.569
People continued to sign up for WhatsApp and
Instagram and Facebook was a stock market

00:00:54.569 --> 00:00:55.569
favorite.

00:00:55.569 --> 00:01:01.629
In mid 2021, Facebook even reached a market
cap of $1 trillion, but unfortunately for

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:05.160
Facebook this seems to have been a significant
top.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.510
Since then, Facebook has shed over 50% of
their valuation.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.650
This correlates to a market cap loss of $584
billion.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:18.800
If you made a company out of just their market
cap loss, your company would be the 9th largest

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:20.930
company in the world.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:24.630
Considering this, I don’t think you’d
be surprised to hear that Facebook is part

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:28.280
of the top 10 worst performing stocks in the
S&amp;P 500.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:32.570
So, how did Facebook go from being a silicon
valley favorite to being one of the worst

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:35.590
performing stocks on the market?

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:40.960
LACK OF USER GROWTH:
One of the leading factors behind Facebook’s

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.350
recent downfall is a lack of user growth.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:48.500
In fact, not only is Facebook’s user base
stagnating, but the first signs of a decline

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:49.980
are starting to surface.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.800
In the last quarter of 2021, Facebook had
a net user loss for the first time in their

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.720
history shedding a total of 500,000 users.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.780
This is extremely bad news for Facebook because
it shows that even Facebook is not safe from

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:04.990
being replaced.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:09.830
Historically, the social media space has always
been a fast moving world as people jump from

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:11.610
one platform to the next.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:16.160
First, there was Friendster, and then we saw
the rise of MySpace followed up by the explosions

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.730
of Twitter and Facebook.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:22.890
After this initial web based era, we saw a
new era centered around apps including WhatsApp

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:26.420
and Instagram and then SnapChat and now TikTok.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:30.810
Considering this rapid evolution, investors
have always been vary of investing in social

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.140
media companies.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:36.490
The next big thing could become yesterdays
news within just a few years.

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:40.690
For example, ya’ll remember Vine, Yik Yak,
or Google Plus?

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:44.150
Clearly, things change on a dime within the
social media industry.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:48.780
Despite this, with facebook, many investors
felt that things would be different.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.950
As industries mature, they can support bigger
and more stable companies to form.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.280
For instance, just take a look at Google.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.800
When they launched in the late 1990s, there
were dozens of different search engines on

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.250
the market and they each had a sizable user
base.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:08.030
But overtime, one search engine after another
fell of the map and Google rose as the winner.

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:12.270
The same thing could be said about the browser
and email spaces as well.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:18.050
Initially, the crown was passed around frequently,
but with time there was only one winner.

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:22.020
Considering this, many investors felt that
Facebook would become the Google of social

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:26.690
media, and Facebok has actually down a tremendous
job at accomplishing this.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:30.800
We just have to take one look at Facebook
stock vs Twitter stock to understand just

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:34.310
how well Facebook has done even with their
recent decline.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:38.590
Despite this strong performance, over the
years, it has become clear that Facebook isn’t

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:41.280
really comparable to Google.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.099
You see, with Google, while they’ve completed
dozens of insane acquisitions over the years,

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:50.280
each of these have expanded the business,
not cannabilized the old business.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:55.730
Google’s original services including Google.com,
Gmail, and Google chrome are all still thriving

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:57.879
parts of there business today.

00:03:57.879 --> 00:03:59.930
The same cannot be said about Facebook.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:04.410
I mean if we’re being honest, who actually
uses Facebook today?

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:08.629
The only reason that Facebook was able to
grow for so long is their strategic acquisitions

00:04:08.629 --> 00:04:10.410
of Instagram and Whatsapp.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:15.030
And this means that if Facebook wants to continue
strong growth, they have to constantly acquire

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:19.500
the next big thing and this is simply not
a very sustainable business model.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:24.120
I think Zuckerberg realized this several years
ago, and that’s why he slowed down on acquisitions

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:27.410
and is instead focusing on VR and the metaverse.

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:32.300
These endeavors have yet to really take off
though, and in the meantime, Facebook’s

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:36.750
stagnating/declining user base is a massive
red flag that investors can’t ignore, and

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:40.060
this is causing them to leave in droves.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:44.960
ATROCIOUS USER SENTIMENT:
Aside from declining user numbers, people

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.360
who are still using Facebook aren’t exactly
the biggest fans of the company.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.860
In fact, the same people often leave comments
on YouTube about how Facebook is the epitome

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:56.800
of evil big tech.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.419
And given Facebook’s history, this reputation
isn’t all that surprising.

00:05:01.419 --> 00:05:05.530
Likely the biggest red mark on Facebook’s
scorecard is the Cambridge analytica scandal.

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:09.610
If you’re not familiar with this scandal,
here’s what went down.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:13.440
The story circles around a British political
consulting firm called Cambridge Analytica

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:17.130
that was willing to go to any lengths to win
votes for their clients.

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:21.340
Executives of the company were actually caught
on camera saying that they were even willing

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:25.389
to use bribes and misinformaiton to help their
clients win votes.

00:05:25.389 --> 00:05:29.460
When this footage went viral, the company
tried to downplay their intentions suggesting

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:32.300
that they would never actually engage in such
activities.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:37.540
But, a whistleblower named Christopher Wylie
would soon prove that this was not the case.

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:41.190
Christopher revealed how Cambridge Analytica’s
associates used a Facebook app to mine the

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:44.030
profiles of 50 million people.

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:48.000
Cambridge Analytica then turned around and
built software that was specifically designed

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:49.990
to influence people’s choices in elections.

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:54.390
I don’t think I need to explain why this
is morally shady and how it interferes with

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:56.479
free and fair elections.

00:05:56.479 --> 00:06:00.490
And though it wasn’t Facebook themselves
that was engaging in such activities, it was

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.080
their platform that allowed this to even take
place.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:08.030
In fact, Facebook themselves confirm that
Cambridge Analytica did not break any policies

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:09.690
when it comes to collecting this data.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:14.770
It wasn’t until they transferred the data
out of Facebook that they broke their polices.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:19.080
As you would guess, people nor prosecutors
were exactly convinced by this reasoning,

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.250
and they would crackdown on Facebook hard.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:25.970
Too often, we see companies that have committed
terrible crimes walk away with measly fines

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:30.630
of $100 or $200 million which is insignificant
compared to their profit.

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:34.949
But with Facebook, the FTC imposed a $5 billion
fine.

00:06:34.949 --> 00:06:39.550
Not only was this a record breaking fine,
but it was 20 times larger than the previous

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:42.270
largest privacy and data security penalty.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:46.630
Now of course, a lot of ya’ll would argue
that even this is not enough for Facebook,

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:49.749
but the FTC did try their best.

00:06:49.749 --> 00:06:54.330
And though Facebook was able to survive this
nightmare, user sentiment has never been the

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:55.330
same.

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:59.270
In fact, even individuals who have literally
made billions from Facebook have turned on

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:02.759
the company including Whatsapp and Instagram’s
founders.

00:07:02.759 --> 00:07:07.020
Likely the most outspoken of this bunch is
WhatsApp’s cofounder, Brian Acton, who straight

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:10.430
up advocates for people to delete Facebook.

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:14.150
Given that such figures have turned on Facebook,
it’s not surprising that stock investors

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:16.430
have also turned on Facebook.

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:20.270
Even investors who are fundamentally bullish
on the company, are cautious of investing

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:24.740
in the stock simply because they don’t want
to hold such a contreversial company.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:29.130
And unless Facebook changes this overwhelming
negative sentiment against them, I think it’s

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:32.530
going to continue to be quite a difficult
uphill battle for them.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:38.240
APPLE PRIVACY CHANGES:
A lack of user growth paired with atrocious

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:43.960
user sentiment is already bad enough, but
the nail in the coffin for Facebook was Apple’s

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:45.310
privacy changes last year.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:50.319
In April of 2021, Apple started giving users
the option to choose whether they wanted to

00:07:50.319 --> 00:07:52.350
let apps track their activity.

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:56.330
And as you would guess, basically everyone
chose to prevent apps from tracking their

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:57.630
activity.

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:02.960
Within just a few weeks of this feature launching,
96% of users opted out of app tracking.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:08.080
And honestly, I think that the 4% that did
allow tracking might’ve pressed allow accidently.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:13.250
And given that 97% of Facebook’s revenue
comes from advertising, this was extremely

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:14.250
bad news.

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:18.539
In fact, Facebook projects that Apple’s
privacy changes will cost them $10 billion

00:08:18.539 --> 00:08:20.250
in revenue just this year.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:23.669
At first, Facebook tried to fight against
these changes.

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:27.870
They tried to convince people to press allow,
and there were even rumors suggesting that

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:31.090
Facebook was preparing an antitrust lawsuit
against Apple.

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:34.770
But, I think Facebook eventually came to a
realization.

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:37.830
This was a battle that they could never really
win.

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:41.959
Even if they took Apple to court and won the
lawsuit, this would just make users resent

00:08:41.959 --> 00:08:45.690
them even more leading to even greater losses
in revenue.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:49.680
Instead, it looks like Facebook has chosen
to take a similar stance to SnapChat’s CEO

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:50.980
Evan Spiegel.

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:54.770
Evan insists that even though these changes
are brutal for the business in the short term,

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:57.580
it’s needed for the industry in the long
term.

00:08:57.580 --> 00:09:01.800
And this is a very important point because
the truth is Apple’s privacy changes last

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:04.010
year are likely just the first of many.

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:09.220
10 years ago, most people didn’t know how
companies like Facebook and Google made money

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:11.300
and frankly they didn’t care.

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:15.410
They were able to use a great service for
free and that’s all they cared about.

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:19.700
But, as more of these quote on quote “free
services” enter the market, people have

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:24.880
become quite aware of the inner workings of
these companies and most people are not big

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:25.880
fans.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:29.850
This has caused privacy to become a focal
point amongst customers and newer social media

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:33.810
companies aren’t focused on being free but
rather being private.

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:35.270
Take Signal for example.

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:39.649
Signal is a non-profit encrypted instant messaging
system created by one of Whatsapp’s cofounders

00:09:39.649 --> 00:09:40.880
Brian Acton.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:45.790
And as of the end of 2021, it has a total
of 40 million active monthly users.

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:49.810
Now of course, this is just a small percentage
compared to WhatsApp’s 2 billion active

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:51.190
monthly users.

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:55.260
But with people like Elon Musk supporting
Signal, I think Signal’s user count is only

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:56.410
going up with time.

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:59.910
Also, I don’t think Signal is just some
anomaly either.

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:04.260
Rather, I think that it’s the beginning
of a trend towards private social media servies

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:08.220
which again spells disaster for Facebook.

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:12.720
META:
Despite all these shortfalls surrounding Facebook’s

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:16.320
social media business, all hope is not lost
for the company.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:19.620
Given the enormous size of Facebook, they
have atleast 10 years to figure something

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:22.140
out and change the direction of the company.

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:26.690
And given that Mark has been working on steering
Facebook away from social media for years

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:29.080
now, it looks like he’s on top of this issue.

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:33.100
He even went as far as changing the entire
company’s name which is usually something

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:35.750
you never want to do for brand recognition
purposes.

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:40.440
I guess he realized that the brand Facebook
wasn’t doing the company any good.

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:43.990
Looking forward, it looks like the main goal
seems to be growing their VR business and

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:48.790
the metaverse which is a pretty solid bet
given that this does seem to be the next big

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:49.790
thing.

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:53.370
It looks like even Apple is betting big on
AR/VR technology.

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:59.040
But, something to note is that even if VR
is the next big thing, there’s no guarantees

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:03.330
that Facebok will come out on top just because
they’re one of the first people there.

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:07.870
After all, Facebook themselves wasn’t the
first social media company in the world.

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:12.550
With that being said though, being large and
first does have it’s advantages, so we’ll

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:13.550
just have to wait and see.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:16.160
Do you guys have a positive opinion on Facebook?

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:17.960
Comment that down below.

